<div class="about-page">

  <section class="about-hero">
    <div class="about-label">About</div>
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="about-sub">Penumbra Forge LLC is a one-person software company, started in 2026. I build developer and security tools that run on your own hardware. Most of the projects are still early — each one says where it stands.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="about-section">
    <h2>What we build</h2>
    <p>The common thread is that the work happens on your machine rather than someone else's server. Three areas:</p>
    <div class="about-grid">
      <div class="about-card">
        <h3>Local AI</h3>
        <p><a href="/umbra/">Umbra</a> is a local-first AI studio for running and inspecting language models on your own hardware, being developed against an NVIDIA DGX Spark. It is early and not usable yet. On this site, the AI tools run a small model in your browser over WebGPU, or point at your own Ollama or LM Studio server.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="about-card">
        <h3>Security software</h3>
        <p><a href="/gate/">Gate</a> scans staged changes for credentials before you commit. <a href="/vexes/">vexes</a> flags dependencies worth a second look across several ecosystems. <a href="/librarian/">mcp-librarian</a> gives coding agents a local, signed skill library. All three are experimental and use heuristics, so expect false positives and misses.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="about-card">
        <h3>Browser tools</h3>
        <p><a href="/tools/">{{ stats.liveTools }} browser tools</a> for security work, encoding, and development. Most run entirely in the page; the few that need the network say so. They are also <a href="/tools/agents/">usable by AI agents</a> through an MCP server.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="about-section">
    <h2>Founder</h2>
    <div class="about-founder">
      <div class="about-founder-body">
        <h3>Shadoe Myers</h3>
        <p class="about-founder-role">Founder</p>
        <p>I'm a security engineer on a corporate incident-response team, where I build the automation and internal tooling. Penumbra Forge came out of a preference for tools that keep your data on your own machine and are open enough to check. That's the whole idea; the projects here are my attempt at it, and they're still works in progress.</p>
        <div class="about-founder-links">
          <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/shadoe-myers" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/penumbraforge" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>
          <a href="mailto:shadoe@penumbraforge.com">shadoe@penumbraforge.com</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="about-section">
    <h2>How we work</h2>
    <ul class="about-principles">
      <li><strong>Local by default.</strong> Most tools do their work in the page. The ones that need the network are labelled, so you know before pasting anything in.</li>
      <li><strong>Few dependencies.</strong> Mostly built on platform APIs rather than large framework stacks — fewer moving parts to trust and keep patched.</li>
      <li><strong>Open to inspection.</strong> The projects are open source, so you can read what they do instead of taking my word for it. No accounts, and no analytics beyond basic traffic counts.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="about-section about-contact">
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Email <a href="mailto:shadoe@penumbraforge.com">shadoe@penumbraforge.com</a> — general, privacy, and security reports alike (see <a href="/.well-known/security.txt">security.txt</a>).</p>
  </section>

</div>

